54 76.12 -15.78 73.96 -15.78 C 71.79 -15.78 70.04 -17.54 70.04 -19.7 C 70.04 -21.86 71.79 -23.61 73.96 -23.61 C 76.12 -23.61 77.87 -21.86 77.87 -19.7 Z M 73.96 -19.7"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 87.09 -11.71 C 87.09 -9.55 85.34 -7.79 83.18 -7.79 C 81.02 -7.79 79.26 -9.55 79.26 -11.71 C 79.26 -13.87 81.02 -15.62 83.18 -15.62 C 85.34 -15.62 87.09 -13.87 87.09 -11.71 Z M 83.18 -11.71"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 85.43 4.02 L 84.36 7.69"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 80.01 14.45 L 77.12 16.95"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 69.81 20.29 L 65.86 20.84"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 57.92 19.66 L 54.68 18.14"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 48.65 12.82 L 46.63 9.64"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 44.39 1.91 L 44.39 -1.91"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 46.63 -9.64 L 48.65 -12.82"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 54.68 -18.14 L 57.92 -19.66"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 65.86 -20.84 L 69.81 -20.29"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 77.12 -16.95 L 80.01 -14.45"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 84.36 -7.69 L 85.43 -4.02"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 82.5 0.78 C 72.65 2.64 66.41 8.02 63.1 17.48"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 79.29 10.15 C 70.09 6.47 61.96 7.65 54.18 13.77"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 71.53 16.28 C 65.82 8.21 58.4 4.8 48.56 5.71"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 61.55 17.25 C 61.18 7.37 56.83 0.47 48.1 -4.14"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 52.98 12.74 C 57.95 4.13 57.95 -4.13 52.98 -12.74"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 48.1 4.14 C 56.83 -0.47 61.18 -7.37 61.55 -17.25"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 48.56 -5.71 C 58.4 -4.8 65.82 -8.21 71.53 -16.28"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 54.18 -13.77 C 61.96 -7.65 70.09 -6.47 79.29 -10.15"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 63.1 -17.48 C 66.41 -8.02 72.65 -2.64 82.5 -0.78"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 72.97 -15.62 C 70.62 -5.95 72.94 1.94 80.14 8.81"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 80.14 -8.81 C 72.94 -1.94 70.62 5.95 72.97 15.62"></path></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>The WL test incorrectly deems these isomorphic.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will use RP to help WL-GNNs overcome this shortcoming.
Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.265em;" width="0.265em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mpadded voffset="6.9pt"><mo mathsize="0.500em" stretchy="false">⇀</mo></mpadded><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}</annotation></semantics></math> be a WL-GNN that we make permutation sensitive by assigning each node an identifier that depends on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Permutation sensitive IDs prevent the RP sum from collapsing to just one term but more importantly help distinguish neighbors that otherwise appear identical. In particular, given any <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in\Pi_{|V|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in\Pi_{|V|}</annotation></semantics></math>, we append to the rows of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{X}}^{(v)}_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{X}}^{(v)}_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> one-hot encodings of the row number before computing <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.265em;" width="0.265em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mpadded voffset="6.9pt"><mo mathsize="0.500em" stretchy="false">⇀</mo></mpadded><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can represent this by an augmented vertex attribute matrix <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left[{\bm{X}}^{(v)}_{\pi}\Join I_{|V|}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">⨝</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left[{\bm{X}}^{(v)}_{\pi}\Join I_{|V|}\right]</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in\Pi_{|V|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in\Pi_{|V|}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{|V|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{|V|}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V|\times|V|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V|\times|V|</annotation></semantics></math> identity matrix and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left[B\Join C\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">⨝</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left[B\Join C\right]</annotation></semantics></math> concatenates the columns of matrices <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
RP-GNN is then given by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}(G)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{|V|!}\!\sum_{\pi\in\Pi_{|V|}}\!\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}\left({\bm{\mathsfit{A}}}_{\pi,\pi},\left[{\bm{X}}^{(v)}_{\pi}\Join I_{|V|}\right]\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mpadded style="width:0.265em;" width="0.265em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mpadded voffset="6.9pt"><mo mathsize="0.500em" stretchy="false">⇀</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo lspace="0.278em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">⨝</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{|V|!}\!\sum_{\pi\in\Pi_{|V|}}\!\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}\left({\bm{\mathsfit{A}}}_{\pi,\pi},\left[{\bm{X}}^{(v)}_{\pi}\Join I_{|V|}\right]\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{|V|!}\!\sum_{\pi\in\Pi_{|V|}}\!\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}\left({\bm{\mathsfit{A}}},\left[{\bm{X}}^{(v)}\Join(I_{|V|})_{\pi}\right]\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mpadded style="width:0.265em;" width="0.265em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mpadded voffset="6.9pt"><mo mathsize="0.500em" stretchy="false">⇀</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo lspace="0.278em">(</mo><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>𝑿</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">⨝</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>π</mi></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{|V|!}\!\sum_{\pi\in\Pi_{|V|}}\!\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}\left({\bm{\mathsfit{A}}},\left[{\bm{X}}^{(v)}\Join(I_{|V|})_{\pi}\right]\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where the second holds since <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.265em;" width="0.265em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mpadded voffset="6.9pt"><mo mathsize="0.500em" stretchy="false">⇀</mo></mpadded><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}</annotation></semantics></math> a GNN and thus invariant to permutations of the adjacency matrix. The following theorem shows that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> in Equation <a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ Can a WL-GNN be more powerful than the WL test? ‣ 2.2 Neural Network Architectures ‣ 2 Relational Pooling ‣ Relational Pooling for Graph Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is strictly more <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">expressive</em> than the original WL-GNN; it can distinguish all nodes and graphs that WL-GNN can in addition to graphs that the original WL-GNN cannot.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The RP-GNN in Equation <a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ Can a WL-GNN be more powerful than the WL test? ‣ 2.2 Neural Network Architectures ‣ 2 Relational Pooling ‣ Relational Pooling for Graph Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is strictly more expressive than the original WL-GNN. Specifically, if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.265em;" width="0.265em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mpadded voffset="6.9pt"><mo mathsize="0.500em" stretchy="false">⇀</mo></mpadded><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}</annotation></semantics></math> is a GIN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2019</a>)</cite> and the graph has discrete attributes, its RP-GNN is more powerful than the WL test.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Equation <a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ Can a WL-GNN be more powerful than the WL test? ‣ 2.2 Neural Network Architectures ‣ 2 Relational Pooling ‣ Relational Pooling for Graph Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is computationally expensive but can be made tractable while retaining expressive power over standard GNNs. While all approximations discussed in Section <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 RP Tractability ‣ 2 Relational Pooling ‣ Relational Pooling for Graph Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> for RP in general are applicable to RP-GNN, a specific strategy is to assign permutation-sensitive node IDs in a clever way.
In particular, if vertex features are available, we only need to assign enough IDs to make all vertices unique and thereby reduce the number of permutations we need to evaluate.
For example, in the molecule <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CH_{2}O_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CH_{2}O_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, if we create node features with one-hot IDs <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C,0,1),\!(H,0,1),\!(H,1,0),\!(O,0,1),\!(O,1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>O</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>O</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C,0,1),\!(H,0,1),\!(H,1,0),\!(O,0,1),\!(O,1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, then we need only consider <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1!\!\!\cdot 2!\!\cdot\!2!\!\!=\!\!4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mpadded style="width:0.160em;" width="0.160em"><mo>!</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mpadded style="width:0.216em;" width="0.216em"><mo>!</mo></mpadded></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1!\!\!\cdot 2!\!\cdot\!2!\!\!=\!\!4</annotation></semantics></math> permutations.
For unattributed graphs, we assign <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\!\!\mod m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.282em;" width="0.282em"><mi>i</mi></mpadded><mo>mod</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\!\!\mod m</annotation></semantics></math> to node <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>; setting <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\!\!=\!\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.816em;" width="0.816em"><mi>m</mi></mpadded><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\!\!=\!\!1</annotation></semantics></math> reduces to a GNN and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\!\!=\!\!|V|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.816em;" width="0.816em"><mi>m</mi></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.716em;" width="0.716em"><mo>=</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\!\!=\!\!|V|</annotation></semantics></math> is the most expressive. More examples are in the Supplementary Material.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>RP Tractability</h3>

<section id="S2.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.1 </span>Tractability via canonical orientations</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ Joint RP. ‣ 2 Relational Pooling ‣ Relational Pooling for Graph Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is intractable as written and calls for approximations.
The most direct approximation is to compose a permutation-sensitive <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.265em;" width="0.265em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mpadded voffset="6.9pt"><mo mathsize="0.500em" stretchy="false">⇀</mo></mpadded><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}</annotation></semantics></math> with a canonical orientation function that re-orders <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\mathsfit{A}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\mathsfit{A}}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CANONICAL}({\bm{\mathsfit{A}}},{\bm{X}}^{(v)})\!\!=\!\!\text{CANONICAL}({\bm{\mathsfit{A}}}_{\pi,\pi},{\bm{X}}^{(v)}_{\pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>CANONICAL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝑿</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mpadded style="width:0.327em;" width="0.327em"><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mpadded></mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.716em;" width="0.716em"><mo>=</mo></mpadded><mrow><mtext>CANONICAL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CANONICAL}({\bm{\mathsfit{A}}},{\bm{X}}^{(v)})\!\!=\!\!\text{CANONICAL}({\bm{\mathsfit{A}}}_{\pi,\pi},{\bm{X}}^{(v)}_{\pi})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall\pi\in\Pi_{|V|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall\pi\in\Pi_{|V|}</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance, vertices can be sorted by centrality scores with some tie-breaking scheme <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Montavon et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Niepert et al., 2016</a>)</cite>.
This causes the sum over all permutations to collapse to just an evaluation of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}\circ\text{CANONICAL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.265em;" width="0.265em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mpadded voffset="6.9pt"><mo mathsize="0.500em" stretchy="false">⇀</mo></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mtext>CANONICAL</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}\circ\text{CANONICAL}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Essentially, this introduces a fixed component into the permutation-invariant function <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with only the second stage learned from data.
This simplifying approximation to the original problem is however only useful if <span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">CANONICAL</span> is related to the true function, and can otherwise result in poor representations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Murphy et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A more flexible approach collapses the set of all permutations into a smaller set of <span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">equivalent permutations</span> which we denote as <span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">poly-canonical orientation</span>. Depth-First Search (DFS) and Breadth-First Search (BFS) serve as two examples.
In a DFS, the nodes of the adjacency matrix/tensor <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\mathsfit{A}}}_{\pi,\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\mathsfit{A}}}_{\pi,\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> are ordered from 1 to <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V|</annotation></semantics></math> according to the order they are visited by a DFS starting at <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, if <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a length-three path and we consider permutation functions defined (elementwise) as <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(1,2,3)\!\!=\!\!(1,2,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mpadded style="width:0.327em;" width="0.327em"><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mpadded></mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.716em;" width="0.716em"><mo>=</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(1,2,3)\!\!=\!\!(1,2,3)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\prime}(1,2,3)\!\!=\!\!(1,3,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mpadded style="width:0.327em;" width="0.327em"><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mpadded></mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.716em;" width="0.716em"><mo>=</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\prime}(1,2,3)\!\!=\!\!(1,3,2)</annotation></semantics></math>, DFS or BFS would see respectively <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="18.14" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 43.7 18.14" width="43.7"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,18.14) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(21.85,0) translate(0,9.07)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 M 0 0 C 0 0 0 0 0 0 C 0 0 0 0 0 0 C 0 0 0 0 0 0 C 0 0 0 0 0 0 Z M 0 0"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M -9.37 0 L -4.41 0"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 4.41 0 L 9.37 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" stroke="#000000"><path style="fill:none" d="M -13.78 0 M -9.13 0 C -9.13 2.57 -11.21 4.65 -13.78 4.65 C -16.35 4.65 -18.43 2.57 -18.43 0 C -18.43 -2.57 -16.35 -4.65 -13.78 -4.65 C -11.21 -4.65 -9.13 -2.57 -9.13 0 Z M -13.78 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -17.24 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">1</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" stroke="#000000"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 M 4.65 0 C 4.65 2.57 2.57 4.65 0 4.65 C -2.57 4.65 -4.65 2.57 -4.65 0 C -4.65 -2.57 -2.57 -4.65 0 -4.65 C 2.57 -4.65 4.65 -2.57 4.65 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_text">2</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" stroke="#000000"><path style="fill:none" d="M 13.78 0 M 18.43 0 C 18.43 2.57 16.35 4.65 13.78 4.65 C 11.21 4.65 9.13 2.57 9.13 0 C 9.13 -2.57 11.21 -4.65 13.78 -4.65 C 16.35 -4.65 18.43 -2.57 18.43 0 Z M 13.78 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 10.32 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.pic1.3" class="ltx_text">3</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
 and <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.pic2" class="ltx_picture" height="18.14" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 43.7 18.14" width="43.7"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,18.14) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(21.85,0) translate(0,9.07)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 M 0 0 C 0 0 0 0 0 0 C 0 0 0 0 0 0 C 0 0 0 0 0 0 C 0 0 0 0 0 0 Z M 0 0"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M -9.37 0 L -4.41 0"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 4.41 0 L 9.37 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" stroke="#000000"><path style="fill:none" d="M -13.78 0 M -9.13 0 C -9.13 2.57 -11.21 4.65 -13.78 4.65 C -16.35 4.65 -18.43 2.57 -18.43 0 C -18.43 -2.57 -16.35 -4.65 -13.78 -4.65 C -11.21 -4.65 -9.13 -2.57 -9.13 0 Z M -13.78 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -17.24 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.pic2.1" class="ltx_text">1</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" stroke="#000000"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 M 4.65 0 C 4.65 2.57 2.57 4.65 0 4.65 C -2.57 4.65 -4.65 2.57 -4.65 0 C -4.65 -2.57 -2.57 -4.65 0 -4.65 C 2.57 -4.65 4.65 -2.57 4.65 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.pic2.2" class="ltx_text">3</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" stroke="#000000"><path style="fill:none" d="M 13.78 0 M 18.43 0 C 18.43 2.57 16.35 4.65 13.78 4.65 C 11.21 4.65 9.13 2.57 9.13 0 C 9.13 -2.57 11.21 -4.65 13.78 -4.65 C 16.35 -4.65 18.43 -2.57 18.43 0 Z M 13.78 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 10.32 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.pic2.3" class="ltx_text">2</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
 (where vertices are numbered by permuted indices), start at <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(1)\!\!=\!\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mpadded style="width:0.327em;" width="0.327em"><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mpadded></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(1)\!\!=\!\!1</annotation></semantics></math> and result in the same ‘left-to-right’ orientation for both permutations.
In disconnected graphs, the search starts at the first node of each connected component.
Learning orientations from data is a discrete optimization problem left for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.2 </span>Tractability via <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>-SGD</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A simple approach for making RP tractable is to sample <span id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">random</span> permutations during training. This offers the computational savings of a single canonical ordering but circumvents the need to learn a good canonical ordering for a given task. This approach is only approximately invariant, a tradeoff we make for the increased power of RP.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For simplicity, we analyze a supervised graph classification setting with a single sampled permutation, but this can be easily extended to sampling multiple permutations and unsupervised settings.
Further, we focus on joint invariance but the formulation is similar for separate invariance.
Consider <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> training data examples <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}\equiv\{(G(1),{\bm{y}}(1)),\ldots,(G(N),{\bm{y}}(N))\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}\equiv\{(G(1),{\bm{y}}(1)),\ldots,(G(N),{\bm{y}}(N))\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}(i)\in{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝕐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}(i)\in{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the target output and graph <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(i)</annotation></semantics></math> its corresponding graph input.
For a parameterized function <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.265em;" width="0.265em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mpadded voffset="6.9pt"><mo mathsize="0.500em" stretchy="false">⇀</mo></mpadded><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}</annotation></semantics></math> with parameters <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}(G(i);{\bm{W}})=\frac{1}{|V(i)|!}\sum_{\pi\in\Pi_{|V(i)|}}\hskip 0.0pt\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}({\bm{\mathsfit{A}}}_{\pi,\pi}(i),{\bm{X}}^{(v)}_{\pi}(i);{\bm{W}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><mpadded style="width:0.265em;" width="0.265em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded></mrow></mrow><mpadded voffset="6.9pt"><mo mathsize="0.500em" stretchy="false">⇀</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}(G(i);{\bm{W}})=\frac{1}{|V(i)|!}\sum_{\pi\in\Pi_{|V(i)|}}\hskip 0.0pt\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}({\bm{\mathsfit{A}}}_{\pi,\pi}(i),{\bm{X}}^{(v)}_{\pi}(i);{\bm{W}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">our (original) goal is to minimize the empirical loss</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$L$}}}(\mathcal{D};{\bm{W}})=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\!L\left({\bm{y}}(i)\,,\,\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}(G(i);{\bm{W}})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>L</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$L$}}}(\mathcal{D};{\bm{W}})=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\!L\left({\bm{y}}(i)\,,\,\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}(G(i);{\bm{W}})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is a convex loss function of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}(\cdot;\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}(\cdot;\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> such as cross-entropy or square loss.
For each graph <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(i)</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample a permutation <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{s}}_{i}\!\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em">∼</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{s}}_{i}\!\sim</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Unif}(\Pi_{|V(i)|})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Unif</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Unif}(\Pi_{|V(i)|})</annotation></semantics></math> and replace the sum in Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ Joint RP. ‣ 2 Relational Pooling ‣ Relational Pooling for Graph Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with the estimate</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}}(G(i);{\bm{W}})=\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}({\bm{\mathsfit{A}}}_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i},{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}(i),{\bm{X}}^{(v)}_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}(i);{\bm{W}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.418em">=</mo><mpadded style="width:0.265em;" width="0.265em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mpadded voffset="6.9pt"><mo mathsize="0.500em" stretchy="false">⇀</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑿</mi><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}}(G(i);{\bm{W}})=\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}({\bm{\mathsfit{A}}}_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i},{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}(i),{\bm{X}}^{(v)}_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}(i);{\bm{W}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">For separate invariance, we would sample a distinct permutation for each set of vertices. The estimator in Equation <a href="#S2.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ 2.3.2 Tractability via 𝜋-SGD ‣ 2.3 RP Tractability ‣ 2 Relational Pooling ‣ Relational Pooling for Graph Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> is unbiased: <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}[\hat{\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}}(G_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i},{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}(i);{\bm{W}})]\!\!=\!\!\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}(G(i);{\bm{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.216em;" width="0.216em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.716em;" width="0.716em"><mo>=</mo></mpadded><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}[\hat{\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}}(G_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i},{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}(i);{\bm{W}})]\!\!=\!\!\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}(G(i);{\bm{W}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i},{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i},{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is shorthand for a graph that has been permuted by <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{s}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{s}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, this is no longer true when <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is chained with a nonlinear loss <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}[L({\bm{y}}(i),\hat{\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}}(G_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i},{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}(i);{\bm{W}}))]\!\!\neq\!\!L({\bm{y}}(i),E_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}[\hat{\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}}(G_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i},{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}(i);{\bm{W}})])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.216em;" width="0.216em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.688em;" width="0.688em"><mo>≠</mo></mpadded><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}[L({\bm{y}}(i),\hat{\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}}(G_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i},{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}(i);{\bm{W}}))]\!\!\neq\!\!L({\bm{y}}(i),E_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}[\hat{\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}}(G_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i},{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}(i);{\bm{W}})])</annotation></semantics></math>.
Nevertheless, as we will soon justify, we follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Murphy et al. (2019)</a></cite> and use this estimate in our optimization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>-SGD for RP)<span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}_{t}=\{(G(1),{\bm{y}}(1)),\ldots,(G(B),{\bm{y}}(B))\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}_{t}=\{(G(1),{\bm{y}}(1)),\ldots,(G(B),{\bm{y}}(B))\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a mini-batch i.i.d. sampled uniformly from the training data <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> at step <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. To train RP with <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>-SGD, we follow the stochastic gradient descent update</span></p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}_{t}={\bm{W}}_{t-1}-\eta_{t}{\mathbf{Z}}_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}_{t}={\bm{W}}_{t-1}-\eta_{t}{\mathbf{Z}}_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where
<math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Z}}_{t}=\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}L\left({\bm{y}}(i),\hat{\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}}(G(i);{\bm{W}}_{t-1})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐖</mi></msub><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Z}}_{t}=\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}L\left({\bm{y}}(i),\hat{\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}}(G(i);{\bm{W}}_{t-1})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>is the random gradient with the random permutations <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\mathbf{s}}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\mathbf{s}}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, (sampled independently <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{s}}_{i}\sim\text{Unif}(\Pi_{|V(i)|})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Unif</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{s}}_{i}\sim\text{Unif}(\Pi_{|V(i)|})</annotation></semantics></math> for all graphs <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(i)</annotation></semantics></math> in batch <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>), and the learning rate is <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> s.t. <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{t\to\infty}\eta_{t}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{t\to\infty}\eta_{t}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t=1}^{\infty}\eta_{t}=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t=1}^{\infty}\eta_{t}=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t=1}^{\infty}\eta_{t}^{2}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t=1}^{\infty}\eta_{t}^{2}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Effectively, this is a Robbins-Monro stochastic approximation algorithm of gradient descent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Robbins &amp; Monro, 1951</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bottou, 2012</a>)</cite> and optimizes the modified objective</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$J$}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>J</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$J$}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\mathcal{D};{\bm{W}})\!\!=\!\!\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}E_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}\left[\!L\Bigg({\bm{y}}(i),\hat{\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}}(G_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i},{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}(i);{\bm{W}})\Bigg)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mpadded style="width:0.327em;" width="0.327em"><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mpadded></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.716em;" width="0.716em"><mo>=</mo></mpadded><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\mathcal{D};{\bm{W}})\!\!=\!\!\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}E_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}\left[\!L\Bigg({\bm{y}}(i),\hat{\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}}(G_{{\mathbf{s}}_{i},{\mathbf{s}}_{i}}(i);{\bm{W}})\Bigg)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\!\frac{1}{N}\!\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{1}{|V(i)|!}\!\sum_{\pi\in\Pi_{|V(i)|}}\!\!\!\!L\Bigg(\!\!{\bm{y}}(i),\hat{\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}}(G_{\pi,\pi}(i);{\bm{W}})\!\!\Bigg)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mpadded style="width:0.049em;" width="0.049em"><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mpadded></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\!\frac{1}{N}\!\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{1}{|V(i)|!}\!\sum_{\pi\in\Pi_{|V(i)|}}\!\!\!\!L\Bigg(\!\!{\bm{y}}(i),\hat{\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}}(G_{\pi,\pi}(i);{\bm{W}})\!\!\Bigg).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Observe that the expectation over permutations is now outside the loss function (recall <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}(G(i);{\bm{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}(G(i);{\bm{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> in in Equation <a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 2.3.2 Tractability via 𝜋-SGD ‣ 2.3 RP Tractability ‣ 2 Relational Pooling ‣ Relational Pooling for Graph Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> is an expectation).
The loss in Equation <a href="#S2.Ex4" title="In 2.3.2 Tractability via 𝜋-SGD ‣ 2.3 RP Tractability ‣ 2 Relational Pooling ‣ Relational Pooling for Graph Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> is also permutation-invariant, but <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>-SGD yields a result sensitive to the random input permutations presented to the algorithm. Further, unless the function <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.265em;" width="0.265em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mpadded voffset="6.9pt"><mo mathsize="0.500em" stretchy="false">⇀</mo></mpadded><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}</annotation></semantics></math> itself is
permutation-invariant (<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}=\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.418em">=</mo><mpadded style="width:0.265em;" width="0.265em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mpadded voffset="6.9pt"><mo mathsize="0.500em" stretchy="false">⇀</mo></mpadded><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}=\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}</annotation></semantics></math>), the optima of
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$J$}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>J</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$J$}}}</annotation></semantics></math> are different from those of the original objective function
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$L$}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>L</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$L$}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Instead, if <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is convex in <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}(\cdot;\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}(\cdot;\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$J$}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>J</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$J$}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an upper bound to <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$L$}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>L</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$L$}}}</annotation></semantics></math> via Jensen’s inequality, and minimizing this bound forms a tractable surrogate to the original objective in Equation <a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 2.3.2 Tractability via 𝜋-SGD ‣ 2.3 RP Tractability ‣ 2 Relational Pooling ‣ Relational Pooling for Graph Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The following convergence result follows from the <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>-SGD formulation of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Murphy et al. (2019)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-SGD stochastic optimization enjoys properties of almost sure convergence to optimal <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> under conditions similar to SGD (listed in Supplementary).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given fixed point <math id="S2.Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}^{\star}\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}^{\star}\!</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S2.Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>-SGD optimization and a new graph <math id="S2.Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> at test time, we may exactly compute <math id="S2.Thmremark1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{{\mathbf{s}}}[\hat{\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}}(G_{{\mathbf{s}},{\mathbf{s}}};{\bm{W}}^{\star})]=\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}(G;{\bm{W}}^{\star})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>𝐬</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐬</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{{\mathbf{s}}}[\hat{\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}}(G_{{\mathbf{s}},{\mathbf{s}}};{\bm{W}}^{\star})]=\overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}(G;{\bm{W}}^{\star})</annotation></semantics></math> or estimate it with <math id="S2.Thmremark1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{m}\sum_{j=1}^{m}\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}(G_{{\mathbf{s}}_{j},{\mathbf{s}}_{j}};{\bm{W}}^{\star})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mpadded style="width:0.265em;" width="0.265em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded></mrow></mrow><mpadded voffset="6.9pt"><mo mathsize="0.500em" stretchy="false">⇀</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{m}\sum_{j=1}^{m}\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}(G_{{\mathbf{s}}_{j},{\mathbf{s}}_{j}};{\bm{W}}^{\star})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.Thmremark1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{s}}_{1}\!\ldots,\!{\mathbf{s}}_{m}\!\overset{\mathrm{i.i.d.}}{\sim}\!\mathrm{Unif}\big(\Pi_{|V|}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em">.</mo></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Unif</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{s}}_{1}\!\ldots,\!{\mathbf{s}}_{m}\!\overset{\mathrm{i.i.d.}}{\sim}\!\mathrm{Unif}\big(\Pi_{|V|}\big)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.3 </span>Tractability via <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-ary dependencies</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Murphy et al. (2019)</a></cite> propose <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-ary pooling whereby the computational complexity of summing over all permutations of an input sequence is reduced by considering only permutations of subsequences of size <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Inspired by this, we propose <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-ary Relational Pooling which operates on <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-node induced subgraphs of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, which corresponds to patches of size <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\times k\times(d_{e}+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\times k\times(d_{e}+1)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\mathsfit{A}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\mathsfit{A}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> rows of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{X}}^{(v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑿</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{X}}^{(v)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Formally, we define <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-ary RP in joint RP by</p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\ \overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}^{(k)}\!\!(G;{\bm{W}})\!=\!\frac{1}{|V|!}\!\sum_{\pi\!\in\Pi_{|V|}}\!\!\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}\!\Big(\!{\bm{\mathsfit{A}}}_{\pi,\pi}[1\!\!:\!\!k,1\!\!:\!\!k,:],{\bm{X}}^{(v)}_{\pi}[1\!\!:\!\!k,\!:];{\bm{W}}\Big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mpadded voffset="-0.9pt"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.158em">∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><mpadded style="width:0.265em;" width="0.265em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mpadded voffset="6.9pt"><mo mathsize="0.500em" stretchy="false">⇀</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mpadded style="width:0.216em;" width="0.216em"><mo>:</mo></mpadded><mi>k</mi><mpadded style="width:0.105em;" width="0.105em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.216em;" width="0.216em"><mo>:</mo></mpadded><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mpadded style="width:0.216em;" width="0.216em"><mo>:</mo></mpadded><mi>k</mi><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ \overline{\overline{\lower 0.86108pt\hbox{$f$}}}^{(k)}\!\!(G;{\bm{W}})\!=\!\frac{1}{|V|!}\!\sum_{\pi\!\in\Pi_{|V|}}\!\!\mathstrut\mkern 2.5muf\mkern-11.0mu\raise 6.88889pt\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle\rightharpoonup$}\!\Big(\!{\bm{\mathsfit{A}}}_{\pi,\pi}[1\!\!:\!\!k,1\!\!:\!\!k,:],{\bm{X}}^{(v)}_{\pi}[1\!\!:\!\!k,\!:];{\bm{W}}\Big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
